-- title:
--   Invertibility of j at generic-fibre points over the cusp
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \ge 1$ and a prime $p$ with $p \nmid N$, and let $c : X \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ be a proper smooth morphism of relative dimension $1$ with $X$ integral, where $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ is realised as the subring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt p`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) of rationals whose denominator is coprime to $p$. The data are: a curve model $M_0$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ with function field $F_N =$ `modularFunctionFieldFull N`, the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the expansions $q \mapsto j(q^d)$ for $d \mid N$, together with an isomorphism $e_0$ from $M_0.C$ onto the generic fibre $X \times_{\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}} \mathbb{Q}$ compatible with the structure morphisms; a section $\varepsilon_\infty$ of $c$; a closed point $x_0$ of $M_0.C$ whose associated place is the $q$-adic place `cuspInftyFull N`; a $\mathbb{Q}$-point $y$ of the generic fibre which is the restriction of $\varepsilon_\infty$ and which corresponds to $x_0$ under $e_0$; a curve model $M_\eta$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with function field `modularFunctionFieldBar N` together with an isomorphism $e_\eta$ onto $X \times_{\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; the Galois-equivariance hypothesis `hgal`, saying that transporting a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point by $g \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ transports its place by `arithmeticGalois`; the compatibility `hcompat`, saying that the place of a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point, contracted along $F_N \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} F_N \cong$ `modularFunctionFieldBar N`, is the place of the closed point of $M_0.C$ it lies over; for each valuation subring $A \subset \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit of $A$, a ring homomorphism $\rho_A : \mathbb{Z}_{(p)} \to A$ lifting the structure map, a curve model $M_A$ over the residue field of $A$ with function field `modularFunctionFieldFullC`, and an isomorphism of $M_A.C$ onto the fibre of $c$ along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the reduction of $\rho_A$, compatible with the structure morphisms; and the reduction hypothesis `hsp`, providing for each such $A$ with algebraically closed residue field a map $r$ of places satisfying `IsPlaceReductionModL A N r` and computing the place of the reduction of an $A$-valued point of $X$ as $r$ applied to the place of its generic fibre point. Finally let $s$ be a closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ such that the image of $x_0$ in $X$ specialises to $\varepsilon_\infty(s)$, and let $P \in X$ specialise to $\varepsilon_\infty(s)$, be distinct from the image of $x_0$, satisfy that the germ at $P$ of the global function $p$ pulled back from the base is a unit in $\mathcal{O}_{X,P}$, and receive a specialisation from the image of the generic point of $M_0.C$. Then there is a unit $g \in \mathcal{O}_{X,P}$ whose image under specialisation to the generic point, the stalk map of $e_0$ followed by the projection, and $M_0.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$, regarded as an element of $F_N \subset \mathbb{Q}((q))$, is the Laurent series `jq`.
--
--   On a proper smooth relative curve over $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ equipped with the modular identifications of its generic, geometric and mod-$\ell$ fibres, this says that the modular function $j$ is regular and invertible at every point of the generic fibre which specialises to the cusp's point in the fibre at $s$ other than the cusp itself. It is used in the construction of a local parameter and $q$-expansion along the cusp section, by [`ModularCurve.exists_algHom_retraction_param_stalk_cuspSection_ffEquiv_symm_eq_ofPowerSeries_isUnit_coeff_one_of_ratCurveModel_compat_of_neZero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_algHom_retraction_param_stalk_cuspSection_ffEquiv_symm_eq_ofPowerSeries_isUnit_coeff_one_of_ratCurveModel_compat_of_neZero).
-- source:
